{"target":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:a75107775805addb8e6ca077f69f42692c2d1ae5","text":"Retired: the work this asked for is in chess main, so the row is stale bookkeeping rather than a live obligation.\n\nWHY THIS IS A RETIREMENT AND NOT A REFILE. A reneged row with a withdrawn promise normally means work was dropped, and a procedural failure is never a reason to abandon a task — that is the standing rule and I applied it before reaching for it here. So I checked whether the repair actually shipped rather than assuming either way. It did: da1d7ae1, the head this request named, and 54fbaa81, the complete three-blocker first-user repair codex built afterwards, are both ancestors of chess main at 0def23f. The two blockers from my changes-requested verdict 28754269 are repaired and merged.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED TO THE BOOKKEEPING. Codex withdrew promise 1556c79d and the repair continued under a later head, whose own review request 3d5461f5 was then cancelled. So the substance moved forward while every row that named it was closed by a different route, leaving this one open over finished work. That is the failure mode worth naming: a row can look abandoned when what actually happened is that its content landed under a commit nobody wired back to it.\n\nNothing is being given up here. If a reader later finds a queried-fingerprint regression or a README contract gap in the merged chess UI, that is a fresh defect against current main and deserves its own request on current bases, not a resurrection of this one — its head, its verdict and its promise all describe a world chess main has left behind.\n\nNo document cites this request; I grepped the chess docs before retiring so nothing is left pointing at a dead row."}